-- title:
--   Compatible pairs, the conditions of Theorem 4.4, and the tractability criterion of Theorem 4.9
-- statement:
--   Let $F_1,F_2$ be nondegenerate symmetric domain-3 signatures of arities $m_1,m_2\ge3$ with nonzero annihilators $\mathrm{Ann}(F_i)=\mathrm{span}(n_i)$. $(F_1,F_2)$ is a **compatible pair** (Definition 4.1, p.40) if
--
--   1. $\langle n_1,n_2\rangle=0$; or
--   2. $F_i=u_i^{\otimes m_i}+v_i^{\otimes m_i}$ ($i=1,2$) with $\langle u_1,u_2\rangle=\langle v_1,v_2\rangle=0$; or
--   3. $F_i=\lambda_iu_i^{\otimes m_i}+\mathrm{Sym}(u_i,\dots,u_i,v_i)$ ($i=1,2$) with $\langle u_1,u_2\rangle=0$.
--
--   For a set $\mathcal F$ of such signatures and a set $\mathcal B$ of binary signatures, the **conditions of Theorem 4.4** (p.44) are: every pair $F_i,F_j\in\mathcal F$ is compatible, and for every $M$ in the multiplicative closure $\langle\mathcal B\rangle$ of the matrices of $\mathcal B$ and all $F_i,F_j\in\mathcal F$, $MF_i$ is degenerate or $(MF_i,F_j)$ is a compatible pair.
--
--   The **tractability criterion of Theorem 4.9** (p.48) holds for $\mathcal F$ (arity $\ge3$) and $\mathcal B$ (binary) if some $T\in O(3,\mathbb C)$ satisfies one of:
--
--   1. $\mathcal F=\emptyset$;
--   2. $T\mathcal F\cup T\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal E$, or $Y^{-1}T\mathcal F\cup Y^{-1}T\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal E$;
--   3. (a) $T\mathcal F\subseteq\mathsf{BG}|\mathsf R$, (b) $T\mathcal B\subseteq\mathsf{BG}|\mathsf R\cup\mathrm{Swap}_{\mathsf{BG};\mathsf R}$, and (c) the nondegenerate members of $(T\mathcal F\cup T\mathcal B)^{*\to\{\mathsf{BG}\}}$ lie in a tractable class of Theorem 2.5;
--   4. $Y^{-1}T\mathcal F\cup Y^{-1}T\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal C$;
--   5. every member of $\mathcal F$ has a nonzero annihilator, and $\mathcal F,\mathcal B$ satisfy the conditions of Theorem 4.4.
--
--   This criterion is the dividing line of the dichotomy (Theorem 9.11).
--
--   **Formalization Note** Item 1 of a compatible pair is stated for all annihilators (equivalent, since the annihilator is at most one-dimensional, Proposition 2.11). Pairs $F_i,F_j$ include $F_i=F_j$. $\langle\mathcal B\rangle$ is the subsemigroup of $3\times3$ matrices generated by the matrices of $\mathcal B$, and $MF$ is $M^{\otimes n}F$. Two readings of Theorem 4.9: Item 4 is printed "$Y^{-1}T\mathcal F\subseteq Y^{-1}T\mathcal B\mathcal C$" and is read as $Y^{-1}T\mathcal F\cup Y^{-1}T\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal C$, as the proof of Theorem 8.13 (p.81) uses it; Item 3(c), printed "$\mathrm{Holant}^*_2((T\mathcal F\cup T\mathcal B)^{*\to\{\mathsf{BG}\}})$ is tractable", is read through the classes of Theorem 2.5 (degenerate signatures are products of unaries, which $\mathrm{Holant}^*$ already contains).
-- source:
--   Cai, Ihm, Group Action and Spin Representation Lead to a Holant* Complexity Dichotomy on Domain Size 3, STOC 2027 submission, arXiv:2610.04750 (held manuscript sha256 b608db44…), Definition 4.1 p.40; Theorem 4.4 p.44; Theorem 4.9 p.48; Theorem 8.13 p.81

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Holant3Dich_Sig
import Definitions.Def_Holant3Dich_IsOrth3
import Definitions.Def_Holant3Dich_Ann
import Definitions.Def_Holant3Dich_InC
import Definitions.Def_Holant3Dich_BoolTractClass

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace Holant3Dich

/-- The standing hypotheses of Definition 4.1 on one member of a pair: nondegenerate, symmetric,
arity `≥ 3`, with a nonzero annihilator. -/
def PairHyp (F : Sig 3) : Prop := 3 ≤ F.1 ∧ F.IsSymmetric ∧ ¬ F.IsDegenerate ∧ HasAnn F

/-- **Compatible pair** `(F₁, F₂)`. Cai–Ihm, *Group Action and Spin Representation Lead to a
Holant\* Complexity Dichotomy on Domain Size 3*, STOC 2027 submission, arXiv:2610.04750 (held
manuscript sha256 b608db44…), Definition 4.1, p.40: "Let F1, F2 be nondegenerate symmetric
signatures of arity 3 or higher such that Ann(Fi) = span(ni) ≠ 0 for i = 1, 2. We say (F1, F2) is a
compatible pair if one of the following holds.
1. ⟨n1, n2⟩ = 0.
2. For some ui, vi ∈ C^3, Fi = ui^{⊗mi} + vi^{⊗mi} (i = 1, 2), satisfying ⟨u1, u2⟩ = ⟨v1, v2⟩ = 0.
3. For some ui, vi ∈ C^3, and λi ∈ C, Fi = λi ui^{⊗mi} + Sym(ui, . . . , ui, vi) (i = 1, 2),
satisfying ⟨u1, u2⟩ = 0."

**Formalization Note.** The hypotheses on `F₁, F₂` are part of the predicate. `mᵢ` is the arity
of `Fᵢ`. Item 1 is stated for all annihilators `n₁, n₂`; by Prop. 2.11 the annihilator of a
nondegenerate symmetric signature is at most one-dimensional, so this is "⟨n₁, n₂⟩ = 0" for the
spanning vectors. -/
def CompatiblePair (F₁ F₂ : Sig 3) : Prop :=
  PairHyp F₁ ∧ PairHyp F₂ ∧
  ((∀ n₁ ∈ Ann F₁, ∀ n₂ ∈ Ann F₂, dot n₁ n₂ = 0) ∨
   (∃ u₁ v₁ u₂ v₂ : Fin 3 → ℂ,
      F₁.2 = tpow u₁ F₁.1 + tpow v₁ F₁.1 ∧ F₂.2 = tpow u₂ F₂.1 + tpow v₂ F₂.1 ∧
      dot u₁ u₂ = 0 ∧ dot v₁ v₂ = 0) ∨
   (∃ (u₁ v₁ u₂ v₂ : Fin 3 → ℂ) (l₁ l₂ : ℂ),
      F₁.2 = l₁ • tpow u₁ F₁.1 + symUV u₁ v₁ F₁.1 ∧
      F₂.2 = l₂ • tpow u₂ F₂.1 + symUV u₂ v₂ F₂.1 ∧ dot u₁ u₂ = 0))

/-- The **two conditions of Theorem 4.4** for a set `F` of higher-arity signatures and a set `B`
of binary signatures. Cai–Ihm, Theorem 4.4, p.44: "1. For all Fi, Fj ∈ F, (Fi, Fj) is a
compatible pair. 2. Let ⟨B⟩ be the multiplicative closure of B. For all M ∈ ⟨B⟩ and Fi, Fj ∈ F,
MFi is degenerate or (MFi, Fj) is a compatible pair."

**Formalization Note.** `⟨B⟩` is the subsemigroup of `3 × 3` matrices generated by the matrices
of the members of `B` (`Sig.toMatrix`); `MFᵢ` is the holographic action `M^{⊗n}Fᵢ`. The pairs
range over all `Fᵢ, Fⱼ ∈ F`, including `Fᵢ = Fⱼ`. -/
def Cond44 (F B : Set (Sig 3)) : Prop :=
  (∀ Fi ∈ F, ∀ Fj ∈ F, CompatiblePair Fi Fj) ∧
  (∀ M ∈ Subsemigroup.closure (Sig.toMatrix '' B), ∀ Fi ∈ F, ∀ Fj ∈ F,
    (act M Fi).IsDegenerate ∨ CompatiblePair (act M Fi) Fj)

/-- The **tractability criterion of Theorem 4.9** for a set `F` of signatures of arity `≥ 3` and a
set `B` of binary signatures: some `T ∈ O(3, ℂ)` satisfies one of five conditions. Cai–Ihm,
Theorem 4.9, p.48: "Holant*_3(F ∪ B) is tractable if there exists some T ∈ O(3, C) such that one
of the following conditions holds.
1. F = ∅.
2. TF ∪ TB ⊆ ℰ or Y^{−1}TF ∪ Y^{−1}TB ⊆ ℰ.
3. (a) TF ⊆ BG|R, and (b) TB ⊆ BG|R ∪ Swap_{BG;R}, and (c) Holant*_2((TF ∪ TB)^{∗→{BG}}) is
tractable.
4. Y^{−1}TF ⊆ Y^{−1}TB𝒞.
5. For all F ∈ F, Ann(F) ≠ 0, and F and B satisfy the conditions of Theorem 4.4."

**Formalization Note.** `TG` is the holographic action `act T G` (on a binary, `T G Tᵀ`), and
`Y⁻¹TG` is `act (Yinv * T) G`. Two readings:
* Item 4 is printed "Y⁻¹TF ⊆ Y⁻¹TB𝒞"; it is read as `Y⁻¹TF ∪ Y⁻¹TB ⊆ 𝒞`, which is how the proof of
  Theorem 8.13 (p.81) uses it ("Y⁻¹TF ⊆ 𝒞 and Y⁻¹TB ⊆ 𝒞. Then F and B satisfy Item 4").
* Item 3(c) is read as: the nondegenerate members of `(TF ∪ TB)^{*→{BG}}` lie in one of the three
  tractable classes of Theorem 2.5 (`BoolTractClass`); degenerate members are products of unaries,
  which `Holant*` already contains. By Theorem 2.5 this is equivalent to the complexity reading
  unless #P-hard problems are in FP. -/
def Criterion49 (F B : Set (Sig 3)) : Prop :=
  ∃ T : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) ℂ, IsOrth3 T ∧
    (F = ∅ ∨
     (∀ G ∈ F ∪ B, InE (act T G)) ∨ (∀ G ∈ F ∪ B, InE (act (Yinv * T) G)) ∨
     ((∀ G ∈ F, Sep 2 (act T G)) ∧ (∀ G ∈ B, Sep 2 (act T G) ∨ IsSwapBGR (act T G)) ∧
       BoolTractClass {h | ∃ G ∈ F ∪ B, h = restrict 0 1 (act T G) ∧ ¬ h.IsDegenerate}) ∨
     (∀ G ∈ F ∪ B, InC (act (Yinv * T) G)) ∨
     ((∀ G ∈ F, HasAnn G) ∧ Cond44 F B))

end Holant3Dich


